// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L1
// @tier: 0
// @tags: types, declarations, generics, arity
// @expected-error: E407
// @description: The interior of a type declaration is checked, so the
//   same annotation gets the same verdict wherever it is written.
//
// ONE ANNOTATION, THREE POSITIONS, TWO VERDICTS — the reproduction:
//
//     let p: Pair<Int, Text, Bool> = …;           refused
//     fn take(p: Pair<Int, Text, Bool>) -> Int    refused
//     type Bad is { p: Pair<Int, Text, Bool> };   ACCEPTED
//
// Signatures and statements were checked; a record field was reached
// through neither. Whatever the checker knows about a written type —
// arity, kinds, meta-parameter refinements, alias saturation — it did
// not apply where a type says what its data IS.
//
// THE RULE WAS ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN, one line above the code that broke
// it: "the lenient fallback exists ONLY for resolution-order gaps
// (forward references that a later pass closes)". The predicate
// implementing it was a whitelist with a single member, so everything
// except one arity error was swallowed. Naming the GAP instead — a
// forward reference is `TypeNotFound` — is both shorter and total.
//
// The forward reference below is the half that must keep working: `Late`
// is declared AFTER the type that uses it, which is a resolution-order
// gap and not a defect. A fix that surfaced every error would refuse
// this file for the wrong reason.

public type Pair<A, B> is { a: A, b: B };

/// Forward reference — declared below, and legal.
public type Holder is { late: Late };

public type Late is { n: Int };

// ERROR: `Pair` takes two type arguments, three were supplied — and it
// is an error HERE, in a field, exactly as it is in a signature.
public type Bad is { p: Pair<Int, Text, Bool> };

fn main() {
    print("unreachable");
}
